Track 2: The Cuatro Turns the Bar Sideways
Story & Context: Inés writes two nearly identical cuatro patterns using the same chord voicing. The first accents a 3+3+3+3 grouping; the second emphasizes 4+4+4. Lucía keeps the exact same harp ostinato beneath both while Mateo refuses to change the electronic drums. The entire sensation of meter therefore changes because of one hand on four nylon strings.

Damián exaggerates the trick by keeping a maraca pattern that can belong comfortably to either interpretation. Midway through the track, Inés alternates the two rasgueados every cycle, creating the illusion of a bar repeatedly rotating ninety degrees. The bridge strips the arrangement to cuatro and echo feedback; when the harp returns, it enters on a note that previously felt like an offbeat but now feels like a downbeat.

